# bytes::Buf::copy_to_slice

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn copy_to_slice(&mut self, dst: &mut [u8])`

Copies bytes from `self` into `dst`.

The cursor is advanced by the number of bytes copied. `self` must have
enough remaining bytes to fill `dst`.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::Buf;

let mut buf = &b"hello world"[..];
let mut dst = [0; 5];

buf.copy_to_slice(&mut dst);
assert_eq!(&b"hello"[..], &dst);
assert_eq!(6, buf.remaining());
```

# Panics

This function panics if `self.remaining() < dst.len()`.

# bytes::Buf::has_remaining

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn has_remaining(&self) -> bool`

Returns true if there are any more bytes to consume

This is equivalent to `self.remaining() != 0`.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::Buf;

let mut buf = &b"a"[..];

assert!(buf.has_remaining());

buf.get_u8();

assert!(!buf.has_remaining());
```

# bytes::Buf::reader

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn reader(self) -> Reader<Self> where Self: Sized`

Creates an adaptor which implements the `Read` trait for `self`.

This function returns a new value which implements `Read` by adapting
the `Read` trait functions to the `Buf` trait functions. Given that
`Buf` operations are infallible, none of the `Read` functions will
return with `Err`.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::{Bytes, Buf};
use std::io::Read;

let buf = Bytes::from("hello world");

let mut reader = buf.reader();
let mut dst = [0; 1024];

let num = reader.read(&mut dst).unwrap();

assert_eq!(11, num);
assert_eq!(&dst[..11], &b"hello world"[..]);
```

# bytes::Buf::take

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn take(self, limit: usize) -> Take<Self> where Self: Sized`

Creates an adaptor which will read at most `limit` bytes from `self`.

This function returns a new instance of `Buf` which will read at most
`limit` bytes.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::{Buf, BufMut};

let mut buf = b"hello world"[..].take(5);
let mut dst = vec![];

dst.put(&mut buf);
assert_eq!(dst, b"hello");

let mut buf = buf.into_inner();
dst.clear();
dst.put(&mut buf);
assert_eq!(dst, b" world");
```

# bytes::Buf::try_copy_to_slice

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn try_copy_to_slice(&mut self, dst: &mut [u8]) -> Result<(), TryGetError>`

Copies bytes from `self` into `dst`.

The cursor is advanced by the number of bytes copied. `self` must have
enough remaining bytes to fill `dst`.

Returns `Err(TryGetError)` when there are not enough
remaining bytes to read the value.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::Buf;

let mut buf = &b"hello world"[..];
let mut dst = [0; 5];

assert_eq!(Ok(()), buf.try_copy_to_slice(&mut dst));
assert_eq!(&b"hello"[..], &dst);
assert_eq!(6, buf.remaining());
```

```rust
use bytes::{Buf, TryGetError};

let mut buf = &b"hello world"[..];
let mut dst = [0; 12];

assert_eq!(Err(TryGetError{requested: 12, available: 11}), buf.try_copy_to_slice(&mut dst));
assert_eq!(11, buf.remaining());
```

# bytes::Buf::try_get_f32

Kind: method
Defined in: `bytes::Buf`
Signature: `pub fn try_get_f32(&mut self) -> Result<f32, TryGetError>`

Gets an IEEE754 single-precision (4 bytes) floating point number from
`self` in big-endian byte order.

The current position is advanced by 4.

Returns `Err(TryGetError)` when there are not enough
remaining bytes to read the value.

# Examples

```rust
use bytes::Buf;

let mut buf = &b"\x3F\x99\x99\x9A hello"[..];
assert_eq!(1.2f32, buf.get_f32());
assert_eq!(6, buf.remaining());
```

```rust
use bytes::{Buf, TryGetError};

let mut buf = &b"\x3F\x99\x99"[..];
assert_eq!(Err(TryGetError{requested: 4, available: 3}), buf.try_get_f32());
assert_eq!(3, buf.remaining());
```

